  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_MINERALNUTRITION_DIAGRAM_Q02 - Nitrogen cycle pools and microbial transformations</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Study the schematic representation of the nitrogen cycle shown below. Identify the processes labelled <strong>P</strong>, <strong>Q</strong>, and <strong>R</strong>, along with the bacteria responsible for <strong>P</strong>.</p><p><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 290" width="420" height="290"><rect x="10" y="10" width="400" height="270" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#cccccc" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="45" y="20" width="330" height="36" rx="18" fill="#fff9c4" stroke="#998a00" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="210" y="43" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="13" font-weight="bold" fill="#222222">Atmospheric N₂</text><ellipse cx="210" cy="120" rx="150" ry="30" fill="#f8d7da" stroke="#a71d2a" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="105" y="120" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#222222">NH₃</text><text x="200" y="120" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#222222">NO₂⁻</text><text x="300" y="120" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#222222">NO₃⁻</text><text x="210" y="142" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#6b111a">Soil 'N' Pool</text><line x1="135" y1="116" x2="190" y2="116" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="190,116 184,113 184,119" fill="#222222"/><text x="162" y="108" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#0055aa">R</text><line x1="235" y1="116" x2="292" y2="116" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="292,116 286,113 286,119" fill="#222222"/><line x1="95" y1="56" x2="110" y2="105" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="110,105 104,100 111,98" fill="#222222"/><text x="65" y="80" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#222222">N₂ fixation</text><line x1="320" y1="105" x2="345" y2="56" stroke="#b32400" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="345,56 339,60 345,63" fill="#b32400"/><text x="348" y="82" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#b32400">P</text><ellipse cx="110" cy="235" rx="70" ry="20" fill="#d4edda" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="110" y="239" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#222222">Decaying biomass</text><ellipse cx="305" cy="235" rx="70" ry="20" fill="#d4edda" stroke="#2d6a4f" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="305" y="239" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#222222">Plant biomass</text><line x1="110" y1="215" x2="110" y2="152" stroke="#006633" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="110,152 107,158 113,158" fill="#006633"/><text x="95" y="185" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" fill="#006633">Q</text><line x1="305" y1="150" x2="305" y2="215" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="305,215 302,209 308,209" fill="#222222"/><text x="315" y="185" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#222222">Uptake</text><line x1="235" y1="235" x2="180" y2="235" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="180,235 186,232 186,238" fill="#222222"/></svg></p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Diagram Interpretation</strong><br/>In the nitrogen cycle diagram: arrow <strong>P</strong> shows nitrate (NO<sub>3</sub><sup>-</sup>) from the soil pool returning to atmospheric N<sub>2</sub>; arrow <strong>Q</strong> shows organic nitrogen from decaying biomass converting into ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>) in the soil; arrow <strong>R</strong> shows oxidation of NH<sub>3</sub> to nitrite (NO<sub>2</sub><sup>-</sup>).</p><p><strong>Step 2: Identification of Each Process</strong><br/>1. Reduction of soil nitrate back to atmospheric nitrogen (<strong>P</strong>) is denitrification, which is carried out by bacteria <em>Pseudomonas</em> and <em>Thiobacillus</em>.<br/>2. Decomposition of organic nitrogen of dead plants and animals into ammonia (<strong>Q</strong>) is ammonification.<br/>3. Oxidation of ammonia to nitrite by <em>Nitrosomonas</em> or <em>Nitrococcus</em> (<strong>R</strong>), followed by oxidation of nitrite to nitrate by <em>Nitrobacter</em>, constitutes nitrification.</p><p><strong>Step 3: Final Conclusion</strong><br/>Process <strong>P</strong> is denitrification (by <em>Pseudomonas</em> and <em>Thiobacillus</em>), <strong>Q</strong> is ammonification, and <strong>R</strong> is nitrification.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>P: Ammonification (<em>Nitrobacter</em>), Q: Denitrification, R: Nitrification</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>P: Nitrogen fixation (<em>Rhizobium</em>), Q: Nitrification, R: Ammonification</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>P: Denitrification (<em>Pseudomonas</em> and <em>Thiobacillus</em>), Q: Ammonification, R: Nitrification</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>P: Nitrification (<em>Nitrosomonas</em>), Q: Biological fixation, R: Denitrification</p>]]></text></answer>
