  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_MINERALNUTRITION_DIAGRAM_Q03 - Root nodule development and infection thread in soyabean</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The diagram illustrates key stages in the development of root nodules in soyabean. Identify the structure labelled <strong>Y</strong> and the event that immediately follows its entry into the inner cortex.</p><p><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 220" width="420" height="220"><rect x="10" y="10" width="400" height="200" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#cccccc" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="30" y="40" width="45" height="140" fill="#e6c88a" stroke="#7a5c2e" stroke-width="1.5"/><rect x="75" y="40" width="45" height="140" fill="#f3dfb6" stroke="#7a5c2e" stroke-width="1.5"/><path d="M120,95 L220,95 C245,95 255,115 235,125 C220,132 215,112 228,110" fill="none" stroke="#7a5c2e" stroke-width="10" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M120,95 L220,95 C245,95 255,115 235,125" fill="none" stroke="#fff8e7" stroke-width="6" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M230,115 C200,102 150,102 120,102 C95,102 80,115 55,125" fill="none" stroke="#b32400" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3,3"/><circle cx="248" cy="105" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="255" cy="115" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="242" cy="128" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="220" cy="102" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="195" cy="102" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="165" cy="102" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="135" cy="102" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="105" cy="106" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="80" cy="116" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><circle cx="60" cy="124" r="2.5" fill="#4a4a4a"/><text x="52" y="195" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#222222">Inner cortex</text><text x="97" y="195" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#222222">Epidermis</text><text x="175" y="75" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#222222">Curled root hair</text><text x="180" y="145" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="14" font-weight="bold" fill="#b32400">Y</text><line x1="178" y1="138" x2="155" y2="106" stroke="#b32400" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="315" y="75" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#222222">Rhizobium</text><text x="315" y="88" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#222222">bacteria</text><line x1="310" y1="82" x2="262" y2="108" stroke="#555555" stroke-width="1"/></svg></p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Diagram Interpretation</strong><br/>The diagram shows a curled root hair containing a tubular pathway (labelled <strong>Y</strong>) through which <em>Rhizobium</em> bacteria travel from the root hair into the inner cortical cells of the root.</p><p><strong>Step 2: Sequence of Nodule Formation</strong><br/>After <em>Rhizobium</em> bacteria attach to epidermal and root hair cells, the root hair curls and the bacteria invade it. An <strong>infection thread</strong> is produced that carries the bacteria into the cortex of the root. Once inside the inner cortex, the bacteria are released from the thread, get modified into rod-shaped bacteroids, and cause the inner cortical and pericycle cells to divide, leading to nodule formation.</p><p><strong>Step 3: Final Conclusion</strong><br/>The structure labelled <strong>Y</strong> is the infection thread, and upon reaching the inner cortex, the bacteria modify into rod-shaped bacteroids and cause inner cortical and pericycle cells to divide.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Root hair hook; immediate death of epidermal cells to release nitrogenase.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Infection thread; bacteria modify into rod-shaped bacteroids and cause inner cortical and pericycle cells to divide.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Vascular bundle; leghaemoglobin is transported out of the root into the soil.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Pericycle layer; root hair straightens and detaches from the epidermis.</p>]]></text></answer>
