  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>NEET_UG_BIOLOGY_MINERALNUTRITION_DIAGRAM_Q04 - Mechanism and stoichiometry of nitrogenase enzyme complex</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Based on the schematic diagram of biological nitrogen fixation by the nitrogenase enzyme complex, which statement accurately describes the stoichiometry, cofactor composition, and operating conditions of the reaction?</p><p><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 460 200" width="460" height="200"><rect x="10" y="10" width="440" height="180" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#cccccc" stroke-width="1"/><rect x="25" y="75" width="45" height="60" rx="6" fill="#fff3cd" stroke="#856404" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="47" y="152" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#222222">Nitrogenase</text><text x="47" y="42" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#222222">N≡N</text><line x1="47" y1="48" x2="47" y2="70" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="47,70 44,64 50,64" fill="#222222"/><line x1="75" y1="105" x2="115" y2="105" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="115,105 109,102 109,108" fill="#222222"/><text x="95" y="96" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#b32400">+2 H</text><rect x="120" y="75" width="50" height="60" rx="6" fill="#fff3cd" stroke="#856404" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="145" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#222222">HN=NH</text><line x1="175" y1="105" x2="215" y2="105" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="215,105 209,102 209,108" fill="#222222"/><text x="195" y="96" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#b32400">+2 H</text><rect x="220" y="75" width="50" height="60" rx="6" fill="#fff3cd" stroke="#856404" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="245" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#222222">H₂N-NH₂</text><line x1="275" y1="105" x2="315" y2="105" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="315,105 309,102 309,108" fill="#222222"/><text x="295" y="96" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#b32400">+2 H</text><rect x="320" y="75" width="50" height="60" rx="6" fill="#fff3cd" stroke="#856404" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="345" y="110" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#222222">2 NH₃</text><line x1="370" y1="90" x2="405" y2="65" stroke="#222222" stroke-width="1.5"/><polygon points="405,65 398,66 402,71" fill="#222222"/><text x="418" y="58" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" fill="#006633">2 NH₃</text><path d="M345,135 L345,170 L47,170 L47,158" fill="none" stroke="#555555" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="4,2"/><polygon points="47,158 44,164 50,164" fill="#555555"/><text x="195" y="182" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Arial, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#555555">Free nitrogenase regenerated</text></svg></p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Step 1: Diagram Interpretation</strong><br/>The schematic depicts the catalytic cycle of the nitrogenase enzyme complex: a molecule of dinitrogen (N<sub>2</sub>) binds to free nitrogenase and undergoes three consecutive reduction steps (+2 H at each step) to release two molecules of ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>), regenerating the free enzyme.</p><p><strong>Step 2: Reaction Stoichiometry and Enzyme Properties</strong><br/>The balanced biological nitrogen fixation reaction is:<br/>N<sub>2</sub> + 8e<sup>-</sup> + 8H<sup>+</sup> + 16ATP &rarr; 2NH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub> + 16ADP + 16P<sub>i</sub>.<br/>Nitrogenase is a Mo-Fe protein (not Cu-Zn) that is highly sensitive to molecular oxygen and therefore requires anaerobic conditions to function (protected by leghaemoglobin inside root nodules).</p><p><strong>Step 3: Final Conclusion</strong><br/>One molecule of N<sub>2</sub> is reduced in three +2 H steps using 8 electrons, 8 protons, and 16 ATP to yield 2 NH<sub>3</sub> and 1 H<sub>2</sub> under anaerobic conditions.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>4.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.2500000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Each reduction step requires 16 ATP, and the enzyme operates best in an oxygen-rich environment.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Two molecules of N<sub>2</sub> are reduced using 8 electrons and 8 protons to yield one molecule of NH<sub>3</sub>.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Nitrogenase is a Cu-Zn protein that remains active in free-living aerobic bacteria even during active photosynthesis.</p>]]></text></answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>One molecule of N<sub>2</sub> is reduced in three +2 H steps using 8 electrons, 8 protons, and 16 ATP to yield 2 NH<sub>3</sub> and 1 H<sub>2</sub> under anaerobic conditions.</p>]]></text></answer>
